<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lalitpur</h1><p class="st-meta">1597 CE – 1768 CE (approx.) · capital Patan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Malla kingdom of Patan in the Kathmandu Valley, whose Durbar Square is among the finest groups of Newar temples and palaces.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal">Patan</a>, an ancient Buddhist city long dominated by its own nobles, was brought under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva-simha-malla">Shiva Simha Malla</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a>, who around 1597 placed it under his son Hariharasimha. Hariharasimha's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siddhi-narasimha-malla">Siddhi Narasimha Malla</a> ruled it as a fully separate kingdom from about 1620, alongside Kantipur and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siddhi-narasimha-malla">Siddhi Narasimha Malla</a>, a devout ruler, built the stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-mandir-patan">Krishna Mandir</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinivasa-malla">Shrinivasa Malla</a> added to the palace complex. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal">Patan</a> was known for Buddhist monasteries and metalwork, and its nobles were powerful, often deposing kings. The city competed with its neighbours for the trade with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah">Prithvi Narayan Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur-nepal">Patan</a> in 1768, shortly after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, and its last ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tej-narsingh-malla">Tej Narsingh Malla</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>. Patan became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a>, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patan-durbar-square">Durbar Square</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-lalitpur.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Lalitpur" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Clemensmarabu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Rajya_Prakash_Malla,_1745_CE,_Newar_script.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1597 CE – 1768 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Patan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Siddhi Narasimha Malla, Shrinivasa Malla, Tej Narsingh Malla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Newar Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Newari (Nepal Bhasa)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Patan, Patan Malla kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lalitpur" aria-label="Kingdom of Lalitpur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6778 1101 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Lalitpur lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7098 1357L7239 1418M7098 1357L7218 1408"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7239 1418h0M7218 1408h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7098 1357h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7098 1357h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Patan. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=kingdom-of-lalitpur">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
